rds, there are two 2s and one Ace remaining. A pair of Kings can only be beaten by a pair of 2s (since a pair of Aces is impossible). If the two 2s are split between the two opponents, my Kings are the highest possible pair and guarantee I keep the lead. If I play a low pair like 33, the Previous Player will almost certainly beat it with 55s or 88s and then lead a single, potentially causing me to lose immediately. Therefore, playing the Kings is the safest and most aggressive play to lock out the opponents.\n\nReason: The Next Player cannot play pairs. I must play my highest pair (KK) to minimize the chance of the Previous Player winning the trick. Only a pair of 2s can beat my Kings; if the 2s are split, I will win the hand by playing my pairs in descending order. Playing a low pair risks losing the lead to intermediate pairs.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠K", "♦K"]}'}
